---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SECTIONS } from "../data/sections";

const title = "How Much Does a NordicTrack Treadmill Cost? (2026): Every Model";
const description =
  "A NordicTrack treadmill costs about $1,599 to $2,999 on Black Friday 2026 ($1,999 to $4,299 list), plus $39 a month for iFIT Pro. Every model priced.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "By Price", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/price/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "How Much Does a NordicTrack Cost?", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/how-much-does-a-nordictrack-treadmill-cost/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much is a NordicTrack treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "At Black Friday 2026 prices, about $1,599 for the T Series 10 or Commercial 1250, $1,999 for the Commercial 1750, $2,299 for the 2450, and $2,699 to $2,999 for the X16 and X24 incline trainers. NordicTrack's list prices run from $1,999 for the 1250 to $4,299 for the X24 (checked November 2026).",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much does the NordicTrack Commercial 1750 cost?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "NordicTrack's US store listed it at $2,499 on 21 November 2026, and the Black Friday price we saw at a specialty retailer was $1,999. NordicTrack itself sold it at $1,999.99 in October, so about $2,000 is its usual selling price. Add $396 a year for iFIT Pro if you will use the classes.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much is iFIT per month on a NordicTrack?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "iFIT Pro, the plan for NordicTrack machines with a built-in touchscreen, is $39 a month or $396 a year and covers up to five users. iFIT Train is $15 a month or $144 a year but is designed for phones, tablets and equipment without a built-in screen (checked November 2026).",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the cheapest NordicTrack treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Among NordicTrack's own range, the T Series 10 at about $1,599 is the cheapest with a touchscreen this month. Retailer-only models such as the T 6.5 S and T Series 5 Starter cost less; Best Buy's NordicTrack Black Friday prices started at $445.75. A used NordicTrack has a median asking price of about $450.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does NordicTrack include delivery and assembly?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "NordicTrack advertises free shipping, which is threshold delivery: the machine arrives unassembled outside your door. Room of choice delivery and white glove delivery with assembly are offered as upgrades, priced once you enter your ZIP code. Delivery takes 10 to 14 business days according to NordicTrack.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What warranty comes with a NordicTrack treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "NordicTrack's product pages list a 10-year frame, 2-year parts and 1-year labor warranty as standard. The Commercial 1750, 2450, X16 and X24 pages also advertise a lifetime motor warranty; the T Series 10 page describes a 2-year motor warranty. A 3-year extended warranty is offered at checkout.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is it cheaper to buy a NordicTrack at Costco or direct?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It depends on the model and the week, and Costco's pages blocked our price check this month. Warehouse clubs often sell club-specific model numbers, so compare the screen, motor and warranty as well as the price. Direct purchases get NordicTrack's $100 off when you add a prepaid iFIT Pro year.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-11-21",
      dateModified: "2026-11-28",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
  ],
};

// "More in" links: the other pages in this article's hub group.
const HERE = "/how-much-does-a-nordictrack-treadmill-cost/";
const HUB = SECTIONS.find((s) => s.slug === "price");
const GROUP = HUB?.groups.find((g) => g.pages.some((p) => p.url === HERE)) ?? HUB?.groups[0];
const GROUP_PAGES = GROUP?.pages ?? [];
const HERE_AT = GROUP_PAGES.findIndex((p) => p.url === HERE);
// The group's first 2 pages (its leads), then the pages after this one, wrapping round, so every page in a long
// hub group gets sibling links and the leads keep theirs (PLAYBOOK §0b L1).
const MORE = [...new Set([...GROUP_PAGES.slice(0, 2), ...GROUP_PAGES.slice(HERE_AT + 1), ...GROUP_PAGES.slice(0, Math.max(HERE_AT, 0))])].filter((p) => p.url !== HERE).slice(0, 6);
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-11-21",
    modifiedTime: "2026-11-28",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "nordictrack treadmill cost",
      "nordictrack treadmill price",
      "how much is a nordictrack treadmill",
      "nordictrack commercial 1750 price",
      "nordictrack x16 price",
      "nordictrack ifit cost",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See Prices by Model", link: "#price-table" }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/price/">By Price</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Price Guide</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">How Much Does a NordicTrack Treadmill Cost?</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        Most NordicTrack treadmills sell for $1,599 to $2,999 this Black Friday, against list prices of $1,999 to $4,299, and the touchscreen models add an iFIT Pro membership at $39 a month or $396 a year. Here is every current model, the real three- and five-year cost, and where each is cheapest.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated November 28, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS<br />
          <span class="ed-method">Based on retailer and maker listings, with prices checked on the dates shown.</span>
        </div>
        <a href="#price-table" class="btn-ghost">Prices by Model</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        Nothing on this page is a paid placement, and it carries no affiliate links. <a href="/editorial-standards/">How we review.</a>
      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          A NordicTrack treadmill is never just its sticker. NordicTrack sells most of its range below
          list price for much of the year, the same machine is cheaper at some retailers than others,
          and every touchscreen model is built around an iFIT membership that keeps billing after the
          machine is paid for. This page puts all three numbers in one place.
        </p>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="On this page">
          <h2>On this page</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#price-table">NordicTrack Treadmill Prices by Model</a></li>
            <li><a href="#t-series">T Series: The Cheapest Way Into NordicTrack</a></li>
            <li><a href="#commercial">Commercial 1250, 1750 and 2450: What Each Step Costs</a></li>
            <li><a href="#incline-trainers">X16 and X24 Incline Trainers</a></li>
            <li><a href="#ifit-cost">The iFIT Membership on Top</a></li>
            <li><a href="#total-cost">The 3- and 5-Year Total for Each Model</a></li>
            <li><a href="#delivery-warranty">Delivery, Assembly and Warranty</a></li>
            <li><a href="#where-cheapest">Where a NordicTrack Is Cheapest</a></li>
            <li><a href="#black-friday">Black Friday and When to Buy</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A new NordicTrack treadmill costs about $1,599 to $2,999 at Black Friday 2026
            prices, or $1,999 to $4,299 at NordicTrack's list prices, and an iFIT Pro membership adds
            $396 a year on top (checked November 2026).</strong> The flagship Ultra 1 sits far above
            that at $14,999, and older or retailer-only models sit below it.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>T Series 10 and 16:</strong> about $1,599 and $1,699 at Black Friday prices,
            the cheapest current NordicTrack machines with a touchscreen.</li>
            <li><strong>Commercial 1250, 1750 and 2450:</strong> list prices of $1,999, $2,499 and
            $2,999 on NordicTrack's US store; Black Friday prices of $1,599, $1,999 and $2,299 at a
            specialty retailer.</li>
            <li><strong>X16 and X24 incline trainers:</strong> list prices of $3,699 and $4,299;
            Black Friday prices of $2,699 and $2,999.</li>
            <li><strong>iFIT Pro:</strong> $39 a month or $396 a year, which is $1,188 to $1,404
            over three years and $1,980 to $2,340 over five.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The practical answer for most buyers is that a Commercial 1750, the model behind most
            NordicTrack searches, costs about $2,000 to buy this month and about $4,000 to own for
            five years with a yearly iFIT Pro plan. If you want the same arithmetic for every kind of
            treadmill rather than one brand, our guide to <a href="/how-much-does-a-treadmill-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how much a treadmill costs</a> puts NordicTrack in context against walking pads, budget folders and commercial gym
            machines.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price-table" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>NordicTrack Treadmill Prices by Model</h2>
<p>
            The table lists the treadmills NordicTrack sells under its own name in the US this
            autumn. Two prices matter for each one. The <strong>list price</strong> is the full price
            NordicTrack's US online store showed for the machine on 21 November 2026. The <strong>Black Friday price</strong> is what 360 Fitness Superstore, a specialty fitness
            retailer that carries the full NordicTrack range, was charging the same day. We could not
            load NordicTrack's main storefront to read its own sale prices, so where we have an
            earlier NordicTrack sale price we say so below the table rather than mixing it in.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Model</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Screen, speed, incline</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">NordicTrack list price</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Black Friday price seen</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">T Series 10</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10-inch touchscreen, 0-12 mph, 12% incline</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">not confirmed this month</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,599 (compare-at $1,999)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">T Series 16</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">16-inch touchscreen, incline and decline</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">not confirmed this month</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,699 (compare-at $2,399)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Commercial 1250</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10-inch touchscreen, 0-12 mph, -3% to 12%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,999</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,599</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Commercial 1750</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">16-inch pivoting touchscreen, 0-12 mph, -3% to 12%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,499</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,999</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Commercial 2450</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">24-inch touchscreen, 0-14 mph, -3% to 12%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,999</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,299</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">X16 incline trainer</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">16-inch touchscreen, 0-12 mph, -6% to 40%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,699</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,699</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">X24 incline trainer</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">24-inch touchscreen, 0-12 mph, -6% to 40%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$4,299</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,999</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Ultra 1</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NordicTrack's flagship</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$14,999</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$14,999</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Hardware figures are from NordicTrack's product pages; prices were checked November 2026
            and will move. Three notes keep the table honest. First, NordicTrack's store also showed
            separate listings for some of these machines at slightly different prices, such as a <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1250-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Commercial 1250</a> at $1,898 and an X16 at $3,499 with a "$100 off with iFIT membership"
            banner, so the figure you see depends on which listing you land on. Second, the T Series
            list prices are missing because the two figures we found disagreed; on 28 October we
            recorded NordicTrack selling the T Series 10 at $1,599.99 and the T Series 16 at
            $1,699.99, the same as the Black Friday prices above. Third, the compare-at prices are
            the retailer's, not NordicTrack's, which is why they do not match the list column.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two common searches are missing from the table on purpose. The <strong>EXP</strong> series, including the <a href="/nordictrack-exp-7i-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>EXP 7i</a>, is no longer part of NordicTrack's own treadmill range;
            some dealers still show EXP listings without a price, which usually means remaining stock
            or a call-for-price. And NordicTrack's retailer-only models, such as the T 6.5 S and the
            T Series 5 Starter, are sold through stores rather than NordicTrack's site and are covered
            under where to buy below. For how the series fit together, start with our <a href="/nordictrack/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack brand guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="t-series" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>T Series: The Cheapest Way Into NordicTrack</h2>
<p>
            The T Series is NordicTrack's entry range, and at about $1,599 for the T Series 10 and
            $1,699 for the T Series 16 it is the cheapest way to get a NordicTrack with a built-in
            touchscreen this month. NordicTrack's page for the T Series 10 lists a 3.0 CHP motor, a
            0 to 12 mph speed range, 12% incline and a 10-inch touchscreen. The T Series 16 moves to a
            16-inch screen and adds decline.
          </p>
          <p>
            The price gap to the Commercial range is smaller than the names suggest. At Black Friday
            prices the T Series 10 and the Commercial 1250 were both $1,599, and the T Series 16 was
            only $300 below the Commercial 1750. That matters because the Commercial machines step up
            in the parts that decide how long a treadmill lasts: NordicTrack rates the 1250's motor at
            3.6 CHP and the 1750's at 4.25 CHP, against 3.0 CHP for the T Series 10. The warranty also
            differs. NordicTrack's T Series 10 page describes a 3.0 CHP motor "with a 2-year
            warranty", while the 1750, 2450, X16 and X24 pages advertise a lifetime motor warranty
            (checked November 2026). Once the cover ends, our <a href="/nordictrack-treadmill-repair/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack repair guide</a> puts a board or motor job with a technician at roughly $300 to $900.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the T Series is good value only when it is clearly cheaper than a Commercial 1250 on
            the day you buy. When the two are the same price, as they were this week, the 1250 is the
            better machine for the money. Walkers who will never run fast can reasonably save the
            difference when there is one; anyone who runs regularly should treat the T Series price
            as the floor of their budget, not the target. Our <a href="/nordictrack-t-series-vs-commercial/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">T Series vs. Commercial comparison</a> goes through the hardware differences in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="commercial" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Commercial 1250, 1750 and 2450: What Each Step Costs</h2>
<p>
            The Commercial Series is where most NordicTrack buyers end up, and the three models are
            spaced roughly $400 to $500 apart at both list and sale prices. NordicTrack's US store
            listed them at $1,999, $2,499 and $2,999 on 21 November 2026; the Black Friday prices we
            saw at a specialty retailer were $1,599, $1,999 and $2,299.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Commercial 1250 to 1750 (about $400 more).</strong> The step buys a bigger motor
            (3.6 to 4.25 CHP in NordicTrack's ratings), the lifetime motor warranty that NordicTrack
            advertises on the 1750 but not on the 1250 page, and a 16-inch pivoting touchscreen in
            place of a 10-inch tilting one. Speed and incline are the same on paper: 0 to 12 mph and
            -3% to 12% on both. For a regular runner the motor and warranty are the reasons to pay
            the difference; the screen is a bonus.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Commercial 1750 to 2450 (about $300 to $500 more).</strong> The 2450 keeps the
            4.25 CHP motor and lifetime motor warranty, raises the top speed to 14 mph and moves to a
            24-inch touchscreen. Those are real upgrades only for fast runners and people who will
            spend a lot of time watching the screen. For everyone else the 1750 already has the motor
            and frame that matter, which is why it is the NordicTrack most reviewers, including our <a href="/best-nordictrack-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best NordicTrack treadmills</a> ranking, recommend first.
          </p>
          <p>
            One pattern is worth knowing before you call any of these prices a deal. In October we
            recorded NordicTrack selling the 1750 at $1,999.99 against its $2,499.99 list price and
            the 1250 at $1,599.99 against $1,999.99, the same figures the Black Friday listings show
            now. NordicTrack discounts these machines for much of the year, so the list price is a
            reference point rather than what most buyers pay. A Commercial 1750 at about $2,000 is
            its normal selling price; a real Black Friday price is one below that.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="incline-trainers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>X16 and X24 Incline Trainers</h2>
<p>
            The X series are NordicTrack's incline trainers: treadmills built to climb to a 40% grade
            and drop to a 6% decline, which no standard Commercial model does. NordicTrack's US store
            listed the X16 at $3,699 and the X24 at $4,299 on 21 November 2026, and the Black Friday
            prices we saw were $2,699 and $2,999, the largest cash discounts in the range at $1,000
            and $1,300 off list.
          </p>
          <p>
            The two share NordicTrack's 4.25 CHP motor rating, a 0 to 12 mph speed range, the -6% to
            40% incline range and the lifetime motor warranty NordicTrack advertises. The difference
            is the screen: 16 inches on the X16 and 24 inches on the X24. At list prices that screen
            costs $600; at the Black Friday prices above it costs $300. Unless you want the larger
            display, the X16 is the one to buy, and the gap is narrow enough at sale prices that the
            choice is mostly about room size and taste.
          </p>
          <p>
            The X16 is also one of the few NordicTrack machines with an exact warehouse-club
            listing: Costco has a product page for it. Earlier in November our general price guide
            recorded the X16 at $2,799 on NordicTrack's site and $2,499 to $2,699 at large US
            retailers, which puts an incline trainer within a few hundred dollars of a <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-2450-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Commercial
            2450</a>. If steep walking is the reason you are buying, the X16 at
            a sale price is better value than the 2450; if fast running is, the 2450's 14 mph top
            speed matters more than 40% incline. Note too that the X machines are heavy, tall when
            inclined and need ceiling clearance at the top of the grade, which is worth measuring
            before you pay for one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ifit-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The iFIT Membership on Top</h2>
<p>
            Every NordicTrack in the table has a built-in touchscreen, and NordicTrack's product
            pages state that an "iFIT Pro Membership [is] required to access full content and
            features on equipment" (checked November 2026). iFIT's own membership page lists two
            plans. <strong>iFIT Pro</strong> costs $39 a month, covers up to five users and is the
            plan made for equipment with a built-in touchscreen from NordicTrack, ProForm and <a href="/freemotion-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Freemotion</a>. <strong>iFIT Train</strong> costs $15 a month for one user and is meant for
            phones, tablets and iFIT-ready machines without a built-in screen. Paid yearly, Pro is
            $396 and Train is $144; Best Buy sells the iFIT Pro yearly subscription at $396 as a
            treadmill add-on. Echelon’s Premier, by comparison, is $399.99 a year, as our <a href="/how-much-does-an-echelon-treadmill-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Echelon treadmill cost</a> page shows.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Plan and billing</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Per year</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Over 3 years</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Over 5 years</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">iFIT Pro, $39 monthly</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$468</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,404</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,340</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">iFIT Pro, $396 yearly</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$396</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,188</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,980</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">iFIT Train, $15 monthly</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$180</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$540</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$900</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">iFIT Train, $144 yearly</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$144</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$432</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$720</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No membership</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$0</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$0</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$0</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Prices are before tax and were checked November 2026; the totals are simple
            multiplication and assume the price does not change, which iFIT does not promise. On the
            touchscreen machines in this guide, Pro is the plan to budget for, because Train is
            designed for equipment without a built-in screen.
          </p>
          <p>
            NordicTrack's store also links the membership to the machine price. Its product pages
            say an additional $100 comes off the order "when you add 1, 2, or 3 year iFIT Pro
            Membership", excluding the monthly plan, and that the iFIT Pro membership auto-renews at
            $39 a month plus tax unless cancelled. So a prepaid year can be worth a little more than
            $396 suggests, but only if you would have paid for that year anyway, and the renewal
            needs a calendar reminder.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whether you need the membership at all is a separate question. A NordicTrack runs as a
            basic manual treadmill without it, but much of what the screen does sits behind it. Our
            guide to <a href="/can-you-use-nordictrack-without-ifit/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">using a NordicTrack without iFIT</a> lists what still works, and <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">is iFIT worth it</a> weighs the membership against what it costs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="total-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The 3- and 5-Year Total for Each Model</h2>
<p>
            Put the machine and the membership together and the real cost of a NordicTrack looks
            different from the price on the product page. The table uses the Black Friday prices from
            the first table and iFIT Pro paid yearly at $396, which is the cheaper of the two Pro
            options. Paying monthly at $39 adds another $216 over three years and $360 over five.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Model (Black Friday price)</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">3 years with iFIT Pro yearly</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">5 years with iFIT Pro yearly</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">5 years, no membership</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">T Series 10 ($1,599)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,787</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,579</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,599</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">T Series 16 ($1,699)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,887</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,679</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,699</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Commercial 1250 ($1,599)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,787</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,579</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,599</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Commercial 1750 ($1,999)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,187</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,979</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,999</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Commercial 2450 ($2,299)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,487</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$4,279</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,299</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">X16 ($2,699)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,887</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$4,679</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,699</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">X24 ($2,999)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$4,187</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$4,979</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,999</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Totals exclude tax, delivery upgrades, extended warranties and accessories, and assume
            today's iFIT price for every year. Two things stand out. Over five years the membership
            costs about as much as the machine: $1,980 is more than the Black Friday price of either
            T Series or the Commercial 1250, and within $20 of a Commercial 1750. And the gap between
            models shrinks once the membership is in: a Commercial 1750 costs 25% more than a 1250 to
            buy, but only about 11% more to own for five years with iFIT.
          </p>
          <p>
            That second point cuts in favour of buying up a model. If you have already decided to pay
            for iFIT, the extra $400 for a 1750's bigger motor and lifetime motor warranty is a small
            share of what you will spend in total. If you have decided not to, the right-hand column
            is your real cost, and the cheapest machine that meets your needs is the better buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="delivery-warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Delivery, Assembly and Warranty</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Delivery.</strong> NordicTrack's product pages advertise free shipping on its
            treadmills and describe three delivery levels (checked November 2026). <em>Threshold delivery</em> leaves the machine unassembled just outside your door. <em>Room of choice delivery</em> brings it into the home and into the room you pick. <em>White glove delivery and assembly</em> brings it into any room, assembles it, sets
            it up and hauls away the packaging. NordicTrack's pages say delivery takes 10 to 14
            business days and that availability depends on your ZIP code. The prices of the room of
            choice and white glove upgrades are only shown once you enter a ZIP code, so we have not
            printed a figure; get the quote before you compare a NordicTrack price with a retailer's.
          </p>
          <p>
            Self-assembly is realistic but not light work. These are heavy boxes, the Commercial and
            X machines especially, and the step from threshold to room of choice is what saves you
            carrying the carton upstairs. Retailers vary: some include delivery to the room, some
            charge for it, some deliver curbside only. Price the machine delivered and assembled,
            not on the shelf.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Warranty.</strong> NordicTrack's product pages list the same standard
            manufacturer warranty across the range: a 10-year frame warranty, 2-year parts warranty
            and 1-year labor warranty. On top of that, the 1750, 2450, X16 and X24 pages advertise a
            lifetime motor warranty, while the T Series 10 page describes a 2-year motor warranty.
            NordicTrack's footnote says warranty terms and conditions apply and points to its terms
            page, so read those before relying on the lifetime figure.
          </p>
          <p>
            NordicTrack also offers a 3-year extended warranty at checkout that, according to its
            product pages, extends cover to a 13-year frame, 5-year parts and 4-year labor warranty,
            and adds a yearly in-home maintenance check for three years. Its price is shown in the
            checkout configurator rather than on the page. The one-year labor term is the reason to
            consider it on a machine you will use hard; on a treadmill used mainly for walking it is
            harder to justify. NordicTrack's pages also describe a 30-day return policy with a
            money-back guarantee.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Financing.</strong> NordicTrack's store advertises 0% APR for 12 or 39 months on
            select products through a TD Bank account, subject to credit approval, with a standard
            purchase APR of 29.99% on new accounts once a promotion does not apply. A 0% plan costs
            the same as cash if every payment is made on time, but check whether the advertised
            monthly figure includes the iFIT membership.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-cheapest" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Where a NordicTrack Is Cheapest</h2>
<p>
            The same NordicTrack can cost several hundred dollars less at one store than another,
            and not every store sells every model. This is what we could confirm in November 2026,
            and where we could not.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Direct from NordicTrack.</strong> The broadest range, the iFIT bundle discount
            and the delivery and warranty options described above. Its list prices are the highest
            figures in this guide, but NordicTrack sells below list for much of the year, so check the
            price on the day against the Black Friday figures in our table.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Specialty fitness retailers.</strong> 360 Fitness Superstore had every current
            Commercial, T Series and X model on Black Friday prices on 21 November 2026, including
            the 1750 at $1,999, the X16 at $2,699 and the X24 at $2,999. Specialty stores are also
            where you are most likely to be able to see a machine before buying and to negotiate
            delivery.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Best Buy.</strong> Best Buy listed seven NordicTrack treadmills, among them the <a href="/nordictrack-t-6-5-s-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>T 6.5 S</a>, T Series 5 Starter, T Series 10, T Series 16 and all three Commercial models, with
            Black Friday prices from $445.75 to $2,299 and a Black Friday sale it said ends 28
            November. Three of its seven NordicTrack prices were under $1,000, which is the cheapest
            way into the brand if you will accept a smaller retailer-only model. It also sells
            treadmill-and-membership packages with the iFIT Pro yearly subscription at $396.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Costco and Sam's Club.</strong> Both list NordicTrack treadmills, including
            models and model numbers that are specific to the warehouse clubs, and Costco has an X16
            product page. Their pages blocked our price checks this month, so we are not quoting
            figures. Warehouse models can look like a NordicTrack range machine but differ in screen,
            motor or warranty, so compare the specification, not just the name. Our <a href="/sams-club-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sam’s Club treadmills guide</a> explains how to compare a club-listed NordicTrack with the same machine elsewhere.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Amazon and Walmart.</strong> Both sell NordicTrack treadmills, but neither page
            would load for a price check in November 2026, so we are not quoting them. If you buy
            there, check that the seller is the retailer or NordicTrack rather than a third party,
            that the model number matches the machine you priced, and what delivery includes.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Used.</strong> The cheapest NordicTrack is a second-hand one. Commonplace's used
            treadmill price index put the median asking price for a used NordicTrack at $450 in
            November 2026, with the middle half of listings between $250 and $800. Those are asking
            prices, not sale prices, and a used machine usually comes without the original warranty.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="black-friday" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Black Friday and When to Buy</h2>
<p>
            Black Friday is the most predictable time to buy a NordicTrack, but the discount is
            smaller than the banners suggest. The Black Friday prices in this guide take $400 off a
            Commercial 1250, $500 off a 1750, $700 off a 2450, $1,000 off an X16 and $1,300 off an X24
            against NordicTrack's list prices. Against the prices NordicTrack itself was charging in
            October, the <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1250-vs-1750/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>1250 and 1750</a> have not moved: $1,599.99 and $1,999.99 then, $1,599 and $1,999
            now. The real Black Friday saving on the Commercial models is therefore any coupon or
            bundle on top, not the headline percentage.
          </p>
          <p>
            The incline trainers are the exception. At $2,699 and $2,999 the X16 and X24 are $1,000
            and $1,300 below their list prices. The X16 figure is within the $2,499 to $2,699 range we
            recorded at large retailers earlier in November rather than below it, so the best X16
            price may still be at another store, but if an incline trainer is on your list this is a
            reasonable week to buy one. Our <a href="/nordictrack-black-friday-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack Black Friday deals</a> page sets a target price for each model and explains how to value a bundled iFIT
            membership in a deal.
          </p>
          <p>
            Outside Black Friday, NordicTrack tends to run sales around the major holiday weekends,
            and the new-year period is when demand for treadmills peaks. If you miss this week, do not
            pay list price: the Commercial models have sold at their discounted prices for most of
            the autumn, and a price at or below those figures is a fair one in any month.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Where People Go Wrong When Pricing a NordicTrack</h2>
          <p>Most NordicTrack overspending comes from comparing the wrong two numbers. These are the four that cost the most.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Treating the list price as the real price</h3>
              <p>NordicTrack sells the Commercial models below list for most of the year, so "$500 off" a 1750 often describes its normal price. Compare the price on the day with what the machine has sold for recently, not with the list price.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Leaving iFIT out of the budget</h3>
              <p>At $396 a year, iFIT Pro adds $1,980 over five years, more than a T Series or Commercial 1250 costs to buy. Decide whether you will pay for it before choosing a model, and add it to the total if you will.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying a T Series when a 1250 costs the same</h3>
              <p>This week the T Series 10 and Commercial 1250 had the same Black Friday price, and the 1250 has the bigger motor. When the gap closes, buy the better machine; the T Series is good value only when it is clearly cheaper.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Comparing a shelf price with a delivered price</h3>
              <p>Free threshold delivery leaves a heavy box outside the door. Get the room of choice or white glove quote before comparing NordicTrack's price with a retailer that includes delivery to the room.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">A NordicTrack costs about $1,599 to $2,999 this Black Friday</strong> for the T Series, Commercial and X machines, against list prices of $1,999 to $4,299, with
            the Commercial 1750 at about $2,000 as the model most buyers should price first. NordicTrack
            sells below list for much of the year, so treat the list price as a reference, not a target.
          </p>
          <p>
            Add <strong class="text-white">$396 a year for iFIT Pro</strong> if you will use it, which makes a
            1750 about $4,000 over five years, and get a delivered-and-assembled quote before comparing
            stores. For the full ranking and the right model for your use, see our <a href="/best-nordictrack-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">best NordicTrack treadmills</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <aside class="ed-more not-prose">
          <div class="ed-more-k">Keep reading</div>
